During review of the diabetes risk factor analysis, I identified several issues that could limit reproducibility across users and computational environments. The original notebook relied on a fixed local file path without clearly documenting how the dataset should be obtained and loaded. Package requirements and versions were not fully documented, data validation was limited, and random sampling operations did not specify a random seed, causing some outputs to change across repeated executions. The notebook also contained generic statistical and machine-learning notation that did not fully represent the variables and analytical workflow used in this dataset.
I revised the workflow to document the computational environment, required dependencies, dataset provenance, and data-loading procedure. I added explicit validation checks for the expected dataset structure, variables, binary outcome coding, and duplicate observations. Statistical notation and explanations were customized to the eight predictor variables and binary diabetes outcome. I also set a fixed random seed (RANDOM_SEED = 42) for stochastic operations so that the participant sample and bootstrap estimate remain consistent across executions. The notebook was reorganized into a sequential workflow from setup and validation through analysis, results, and conclusions.
Finally, I verified reproducibility by restarting the Google Colab session and executing the complete notebook from top to bottom. All cells executed successfully, statistical results remained consistent, and the expected tables, figures, and analytical outputs were reproduced.
